[[General elections]] were held in [[Chile]] on 21 November 2021, including presidential, parliamentary and regional elections. Voters went to the polls to elect a [[President of Chile|president]] to serve a four-year term, 27 of 50 members of the [[Senate of Chile|Senate]] to serve an eight-year term, all 155 members of the [[Chamber of Deputies of Chile|Chamber of Deputies]] to serve a four-year term and all 302 members of the regional boards to serve a three-year term.{{Efn|It was the last opportunity in which the regional boards members are elected together with the presidential and parliamentary elections, since from the next election, scheduled for October 2024, they will be elected together with the mayors, councilors and regional governors, being the first election of the latter in April 2021. Therefore, the term of the regional boards members elected in 2021 will last just under three years, between 11 March 2022 and 6 January 2025.}} Following an electoral reform in 2015, the Senate increased its membership from 38 to 43 in 2017 and grew to its full size of 50 seats after this election.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.latercera.com/noticia/sistema-electoral-pasa-prueba-fuego/|title=El sistema electoral pasa su prueba de fuego|date=20 November 2017|publisher=La Tercera|language=es-CL|access-date=29 January 2021|archive-date=8 March 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210308090321/https://www.latercera.com/noticia/sistema-electoral-pasa-prueba-fuego/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Despite narrowly trailing conservative candidate [[José Antonio Kast]] in the first round of the presidential election, leftist candidate [[Gabriel Boric]] emerged as the winner of the second round with 56% of the vote, a larger margin than predicted by opinion polls.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Former student activist wins Chile's presidential election |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/12/20/gabriel-boric-wins-chiles-presidential-election |access-date=2022-01-01 |website=www.aljazeera.com |language=en}}</ref> Kast conceded defeat shortly after voting ended.<ref>{{cite news |date=2021-12-19 |title=Leftist Boric set to become new Chile president as Kast concedes defeat |publisher=Deutsche Welle |url=https://www.dw.com/en/leftist-boric-set-to-become-new-chile-president-as-kast-concedes-defeat/a-60187979 |url-status=live |accessdate=2021-12-19 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211219232025/https://www.dw.com/en/leftist-boric-set-to-become-new-chile-president-as-kast-concedes-defeat/a-60187979 |archive-date=19 December 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=20 December 2021 |title=Leftist Gabriel Boric wins Chile presidential election |publisher=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-59715941 |url-status=live |access-date=20 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211220000309/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-59715941 |archive-date=20 December 2021}}</ref> At the age of 35, Boric became the youngest president ever elected in Chile and also set a record for receiving the highest number of votes in Chilean history. The turnout in the second round increased to 56%, the highest since voting became voluntary in Chile in 2013.

In the parliamentary elections the center-right coalition [[Chile Podemos Más]] remained the largest bloc in both chambers and increased their number of senators, despite seeing their vote share fall by more than 10 percentage points compared to the previous election. On the left, the new coalition [[Apruebo Dignidad]] saw gains at the expense of the center-left [[New Social Pact]] (NPS), becoming the second largest bloc in the Chamber of Deputies. However, NPS won more seats in the Senate. New parties, including the far-right [[Republican Party (Chile, 2019)|Republican Party]] and the populist [[Party of the People (Chile)|Party of the People]], also gained several seats. Consequently, the newly elected Congress was split evenly between the combined left and right,{{Efn|The combined left coalition, including [[Apruebo Dignidad]], [[New Social Pact]], [[Dignidad Ahora]] and the [[Green Ecologist Party (Chile)|Green Ecologist Party]] had 79 deputies and 24 senators.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.elmostrador.cl/dia/2022/01/12/senadora-electa-fabiola-campillai-se-sumara-a-bancada-de-apruebo-dignidad/ | title=Senadora electa Fabiola Campillai se sumará a bancada de Apruebo Dignidad | date=12 January 2022 }}</ref> The combined right coalition, including [[Chile Podemos Más]] and the [[Republican Party (Chile, 2019)|Republican Party]] had 68 deputies and 25 senators. Eight deputies and two senators are not considered part of any coalition.}} with the non-aligned congresspeople holding the balance of power.<ref>{{cite web |date=2021-11-22 |title=Chile Elections 2021: The right wing reaches the highest representation in the Chilean Senate since the return of democracy {{!}} International |url=https://marketresearchtelecast.com/chile-elections-2021-the-right-wing-reaches-the-highest-representation-in-the-chilean-senate-since-the-return-of-democracy-international/208848/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211209195114/https://marketresearchtelecast.com/chile-elections-2021-the-right-wing-reaches-the-highest-representation-in-the-chilean-senate-since-the-return-of-democracy-international/208848/ |archive-date=9 December 2021 |access-date=2021-12-08 |website=Market Research Telecast |language=en-US}}</ref>

On 11 March 2022 all the newly elected authorities, including president-elect Boric, took office.<ref>{{cite web |last=Cuffe |first=Sandra |title=Chileans vote for new president to lead transition period |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/11/21/chileans-vote-for-president-to-lead-transition-period |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211207002503/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/11/21/chileans-vote-for-president-to-lead-transition-period |archive-date=7 December 2021 |access-date=2021-12-08 |website=www.aljazeera.com |language=en}}</ref>

==Analysis==
The 2021 Chilean election cycle was notable for its polarization, representing a departure from the country's political normality. Against the backdrop of the [[2019–2021 Chilean protests|2019 protests]], the [[Constitutional Convention (Chile)|writing of a new constitution]], and the [[COVID-19 pandemic]],<ref>{{Cite news|title=Analysis {{!}} Chile's millennial president-elect is a sign of a very different 'pink tide'|language=en-US|newspaper=Washington Post|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2021/12/22/chile-boric-left-latin-america/|access-date=2022-01-01|issn=0190-8286}}</ref> the dominant center-left and center-right coalitions that had ruled the country since the end of the [[Military dictatorship of Chile (1973–1990)|military dictatorship]] experienced a significant drop in support in the elections for [[2021 Chilean municipal elections|mayors]], [[2021 Chilean gubernatorial elections|governors]], and [[2021 Chilean Constitutional Convention election|members of the Constitutional Convention]] held on May 15 and 16.

In the subsequent presidential election, candidates from the traditional centrist coalitions finished in fourth and fifth place, while two candidates from newly formed parties and coalitions, Kast and Boric, qualified for the second round.<ref>{{cite web|date=2021-11-22|title=Ex-protester, far-right lawmaker to meet in Chilean runoff|url=https://apnews.com/article/business-elections-presidential-elections-south-america-chile-86e72bcd0193207f79479ce9cb726092|access-date=2021-12-08|website=AP NEWS|language=en|archive-date=8 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211208181110/https://apnews.com/article/business-elections-presidential-elections-south-america-chile-86e72bcd0193207f79479ce9cb726092|url-status=live}}</ref> This represented a departure from the country's political history, which had repeatedly seen power bounce between center-right and center-left parties with little structural movement between presidents.

The desire for change among voters led them to reject the traditional political parties and support candidates from new parties. Kast of the far-right [[Republican Party (Chile, 2019)|Republican Party]] ran a campaign on populist and Christian conservative values, emphasizing "law and order" and drawing comparisons with US president [[Donald Trump]] and former Brazilian president [[Jair Bolsonaro]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=2021-12-19|title=Leftist millennial wins election as Chile's next president|url=https://apnews.com/article/elections-caribbean-donald-trump-chile-santiago-5fc78a1fe1cb26a06839e8a7b59c8730|access-date=2022-01-01|website=AP NEWS|language=en}}</ref> In contrast, Boric, a member of the left-wing {{lang|es|italic=no|[[Apruebo Dignidad]]}} coalition, campaigned on a progressive message reflecting the ideas behind the 2019 protests. He advocated for broadening the social safety net, higher taxes on the wealthy, combating climate change, social justice, and change to the current privatized pension system. Boric also supported the ongoing writing of a new Chilean constitution, while Kast repeatedly said he would interfere to stop its ratification if elected.

==Electoral system==
{{Politics of Chile}}

The President is elected using the [[two-round system]]; if no candidate receives a majority of the vote in the first round, a second round will be held.<ref>{{cite web|title=IFES Election Guide {{!}} Elections: Chile President 2017|url=https://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2522/|access-date=14 February 2021|website=www.electionguide.org|archive-date=3 December 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201203194545/https://electionguide.org/elections/id/2522/|url-status=live}}</ref>

The 50 members of the Senate are elected for eight-year terms, with around half of the Senators renewed at each general election. Senators are elected from 16 multi-member constituencies of between two and five seats based on the [[regions of Chile|regions]]. Seats are filled by party list [[proportional representation]]. Each voter votes for one party list or list of a coalition. Seats are allocated by the [[d'Hondt method]].The 2021 elections saw 27 members elected, representing the regions of [[Antofagasta Region|Antofagasta]], [[Biobío Region|Biobío]], [[Coquimbo Region|Coquimbo]], [[O'Higgins Region|O'Higgins]], [[Los Lagos Region|Los Lagos]], [[Los Ríos Region|Los Ríos]], [[Magallanes Region|Magallanes]] and [[Santiago Metropolitan Region]]. (Vacancies of seats arising between general elections are filled by a person selected by the same party.)<ref>{{cite web |title=IPU PARLINE database: CHILE (Senado), Electoral system |url=http://archive.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2064_B.htm |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200913094313/http://archive.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2064_B.htm |archive-date=13 September 2020 |access-date=14 February 2021 |website=archive.ipu.org}}</ref><ref name="eg">{{Cite web | title=IFES Election Guide {{!}} Elections: Chilean Chamber of Deputies 2021 General | url=https://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/3607/ | access-date=2026-09-10 | website=www.electionguide.org}}</ref>

In the National Congress, the 155 members of the [[Chamber of Deputies (Chile)|Chamber of Deputies]] are elected from 28 multi-member constituencies with between three and eight seats by party list [[proportional representation]]. Each voter votes for one party list or list of a coalition. Seats are allocated by the [[d'Hondt method]]. (Vacancies of seats arising between general elections are filled by a person selected by the same party.)<ref>{{cite web|title=IPU PARLINE database: CHILE (Cámara de Diputados), Electoral system|url=http://archive.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2063_B.htm|access-date=14 February 2021|website=archive.ipu.org|archive-date=13 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200913093908/http://archive.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2063_B.htm|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="eg"/>

