==Public transport inefficiency==
On the day of the second round on 19 December, voters at bus stops in rural parts of the country, and large municipalities in the [[Santiago Metropolitan Region]], such as [[Puente Alto]], [[San Bernardo, Chile|San Bernardo]], and [[Maipú, Chile|Maipú]], waited hours for public bus services in the blazing sun to reach their polling stations, due to a shortage of public bus services available on that day.

Soon after these reports came in, local authorities and citizens in these municipalities took to social media to show [[Red Metropolitana de Movilidad]] bus terminals and parking decks full of unused city buses. This led to speculation that the incumbent government was suppressing voters by reducing bus services to prevent them from casting their votes. Apparently, most complaints of delays came from neighborhoods where Gabriel Boric had stronger levels of support.

Leading figures from Boric's campaign, such as [[Izkia Siches]] cried foul, accusing the government of trying to help Kast win the election. Boric said "the government has a responsibility" to solve the problem to allow voters to be ferried to vote with the unused buses. The elections authority Servel expressed similar concerns to Transport Minister [[Gloria Hutt]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://chiletoday.cl/chileans-denounce-lack-of-public-transport-amid-historic-elections/|title=Chileans denounce lack of buses amid historic elections|website=Chile Today|date=December 19, 2021|accessdate=December 20, 2021|archive-date=19 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211219192607/https://chiletoday.cl/chileans-denounce-lack-of-public-transport-amid-historic-elections/|url-status=live}}</ref>

The government spokesman [[Jaime Bellolio]] called the Boric campaign's claim as a "blatant lie", and denied that the government was suppressing voters, claiming that there were between 5,000 and 6,000 buses running in the Metropolitan Region. However, this was contradicted by transport authorities, which stated that only 3,000 buses were operating. The transport authority however, also stressed that the number of buses was 55 percent more than a usual Sunday and between 3 percent or 4 percent greater than on the first round of election in November.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.biobiochile.cl/noticias/nacional/chile/2021/12/19/elecciones-chile-gobierno-desmintio-acusaciones-del-comando-de-boric-por-falta-de-buses.shtml|title=Gobierno acusa a comando de Boric de "mentir descaradamente" tras aludir intención en falta de buses|website=biobiochile|date=December 19, 2021|accessdate=December 20, 2021|language=es|archive-date=20 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211220064211/https://www.biobiochile.cl/noticias/nacional/chile/2021/12/19/elecciones-chile-gobierno-desmintio-acusaciones-del-comando-de-boric-por-falta-de-buses.shtml|url-status=live}}</ref>

A Transantiago bus driver claimed that only 40 percent of the bus drivers available were driving that day, despite statements from the government that they were operating buses on a normal work schedule. As a result, carpools were organized through social media, while private services like [[Uber]] and [[Cabify]] offered ride discounts for voters to travel to polling stations.<ref name="biobiochile">{{cite web|url=https://www.biobiochile.cl/noticias/nacional/region-metropolitana/2021/12/19/falto-mucha-gente-choferes-del-transantiago-acusan-que-colegas-no-trabajaron-en-segunda-vuelta.shtml|title="Pudimos haber sido lentos en reaccionar": Hutt realiza mea culpa tras acusación por falta de buses|website=biobiochile|date=December 19, 2021|accessdate=December 20, 2021|language=es|archive-date=20 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211220064225/https://www.biobiochile.cl/noticias/nacional/region-metropolitana/2021/12/19/falto-mucha-gente-choferes-del-transantiago-acusan-que-colegas-no-trabajaron-en-segunda-vuelta.shtml|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the evening, just before polls closed, Transport Minister Gloria Hutt apologized, acknowledging the government was slow to react to the situation and blamed the delays on road work and traffic, but denied that the government was engaging in voter suppression.<ref name="biobiochile"/>

== Results ==

===President===
Turnout for the second round rose by 1.2 million from the first round, and from 47.3% in the first round to 55.6%, the highest level for any Chilean election since voting ceased to be compulsory in 2012.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/20/gabriel-boric-vows-to-fight-privileges-of-the-few-as-chiles-premier|title=Gabriel Boric vows to 'fight privileges of the few' as Chile's premier|website=The Guardian|date=December 19, 2021|accessdate=December 20, 2021|archive-date=20 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211220170029/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/20/gabriel-boric-vows-to-fight-privileges-of-the-few-as-chiles-premier|url-status=live}}</ref>

Gabriel Boric won the election with 55.9% of the vote and became the youngest president in Chile's history and [[List of state leaders by age#Youngest serving state leaders|youngest state leader in the world]].<ref name="bbc 2021">{{Cite news|date=2021-12-20|title=Leftist Gabriel Boric wins Chile presidential election|language=en-GB|work=BBC News|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-59715941|access-date=2021-12-20|archive-date=20 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211220000309/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-59715941|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="yahoo 2021">{{cite web|title=Leftist lawmaker Boric wins polarized election in Chile, to become nation's youngest president|url=https://news.yahoo.com/leftist-lawmaker-boric-wins-polarized-000051533.html|access-date=2021-12-20|website=news.yahoo.com|date=20 December 2021 |language=en-US}}</ref>

A post-election survey showed that despite their endorsement of Kast, 59% of Parisi's voters and 23% of Sichel's voters voted for Boric, while 82% of Yasna Provoste's supporters, 83% of Marco Enríquez-Ominami's supporters, and 92% of Eduardo Artés's supporters voted for Boric.<ref>{{cite web|title=Pulso Ciudadano: Un 59% de los votantes de Parisi y un 23% de los de Sichel optaron por Boric en segunda vuelta|url=https://www.cnnchile.com/elecciones2021/pulso-ciudadano-votantes-parisi-sichel-segunda-vuelta_20211226/|access-date=2021-12-30|website=CNN Chile|language=es}}</ref>

